      "annotatedScript": "[STILLNESS] On the second Tuesday of October, the rain in New Orleans fell warm and sideways, turning the cobblestones on Frenchman Street into dark mirrors. [BEAT] Inside a narrow upstairs apartment above a shuttered print shop, fifty-four-year-old Marcus Vance sat at his kitchen table with a brass Bach Stradivarius trumpet resting across his knees and a single sheet of clinic paper propped against the sugar bowl. [PAUSE 1.5s] The paper came from the dental prosthetics clinic on Canal Street. At the bottom, highlighted in pale green, was a number: [EMPHASIS: ninety-four percent functional recovery].\n\nMarcus had spent thirty-one years making his living with his upper lip pressed against a silver mouthpiece. Every Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday night, he walked three blocks to The Spotted Cat, climbed onto the low wooden riser, and played four sets of traditional jazz until his shirt stuck to his ribs. [LEAN IN] Six weeks earlier, a delivery van backing out of an alley on Royal Street had clipped the front wheel of his bicycle. Marcus went over the handlebars, caught the curb with his jaw, and lost two teeth on the upper left side\u2014his lateral incisor and his canine. [PAUSE 1.5s] The clinic had fitted him with a temporary acrylic bridge, measured his bite force with a digital sensor, and declared his rehabilitation ahead of schedule. Ninety-four percent. On a quarterly outcomes dashboard, Marcus Vance was a success story. [LOWER VOICE] Yet when he lifted the horn to his mouth in the quiet kitchen, blew a steady stream of air, and tried to center a middle G, the note did not speak. Instead, a thin, papery hiss leaked out the left corner of his lip, and the brass stayed dead.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [SPEED UP] The following afternoon, Marcus carried his case down the stairs anyway and walked through the drizzle to the rehearsal room behind the club. Ray Baptiste, the bandleader and tuba player, was already there, wiping condensation off his bell with a faded towel. Ray took one look at Marcus's face, then at the trumpet case, and did not smile. [BEAT] \"Doc say you're cleared?\" Ray asked, his voice carrying the dry rumble of a man who had seen forty years of musicians lie about their health. \"She said ninety-four percent,\" Marcus answered, snapping the latches open. \"Bite force is back. Swelling's down.\" Ray set his towel on a folding chair. \"Bite force don't play a second-line chorus, Marcus. Show me.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nMarcus raised the horn, set the rim against his upper lip, and inhaled. He knew the physics of an embouchure better than he knew the street grid of his own neighborhood: the teeth behind the lip act like a marble wall, giving the muscle something firm to brace against so the aperture stays tight and round. With the acrylic bridge, the wall felt spongy, a fraction of a millimeter too thick, like trying to balance a coin on a wet sponge. He pushed air. [SLOW DOWN] The first two beats of \"Bourbon Street Parade\" came out flat and breathy, and on the third beat, as he reached up for the high C, the seal broke completely. Air hissed past his left cheek. The horn squeaked, then died. [PAUSE 2s] Nobody in the room moved. Ray picked up his towel again. \"You're forcing it,\" Ray said quietly. \"You're pinching the right side to make up for the left. You do that for forty-five minutes on a Friday night, you'll tear your lip muscle and be out six months instead of six weeks.\" Marcus stared at the floor. \"I've got rent on the first, Ray. The clinic says I'm almost whole.\" Ray shook his head. [EMPHASIS: \"The clinic measures chewing, brother. They don't measure Tuesday nights.\"]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] By eleven o'clock that night, the apartment felt smaller than ever. Marcus sat under a single yellow lamp, a metronome ticking softly at sixty beats per minute on the windowsill. For two hours he had tried every trick in his repertoire\u2014shifting the mouthpiece a hair to the right, wetting the rim, angling the bell downward to take pressure off the upper jaw. Each time, his lip swelled a little more, and each time, the hiss returned. [BEAT] On the table beside the clinic sheet lay a letter from the regional arts council's health fund, notifying him that his temporary disability stipend would end on Friday because his provider's quarterly report logged him as \"functionally restored.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Marcus picked up the pen, stared at the number ninety-four, and felt a cold, unfamiliar tightness in his chest. On paper, he was an outlier who had already crossed the finish line. In the room, he could not play a simple major scale without sounding like a beginner. [STILLNESS] He set the horn in its velvet cradle, closed the lid, and sat in the dark listening to the rain hit the tin awning outside.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [WARM SMILE] The next morning, Marcus did not go back to the rehearsal room. He walked to Canal Street, carried his trumpet case into the prosthetics clinic, and asked to see Dr. Pauline Chen without an appointment. When she stepped into the exam room twenty minutes later, holding his chart, she looked surprised. \"Mr. Vance, your numbers were excellent last week,\" she said, tapping the tablet. \"Your occlusion score is in the top decile.\" [LEAN IN] Marcus did not argue with the tablet. Instead, he opened the case, pulled out only the silver mouthpiece\u2014not the whole horn\u2014and held it out to her. \"Dr. Chen,\" he said, \"I need you to watch what happens when I blow.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] He pressed the mouthpiece to his lip and blew a steady stream of air. The hiss was unmistakable. Then he slipped two fingers into his mouth, pulled back his cheek, and pointed to the tiny seam where the temporary acrylic met his natural gumline. \"Your sensor measures how hard I can bite down,\" Marcus said evenly. \"It doesn't measure [EMPHASIS: two millimeters of porcelain] sitting where my lip needs to seal. On your chart, I'm ninety-four percent. Out there, I'm zero.\"\n\n[SLOW DOWN] Dr. Chen set the tablet down on the counter. For a long moment, she did not look at the screen at all. She put on her loupes, leaned in under the exam light, and asked Marcus to buzz his lips without the mouthpiece, then with it, watching the left corner of his mouth tremble as the air escaped. [BEAT] \"We calibrated the bridge for vertical load,\" she murmured, mostly to herself. \"We never tested lateral seal under air pressure.\" She reached for a fine diamond bur, looked at Marcus, and asked, \"If I shave a millimeter off the labial face right now, will you blow again?\" [PAUSE 1.5s] For the next forty minutes, there were no charts, no percentages, and no quarterly targets in that room\u2014only a dentist, a trumpeter, a silver mouthpiece, and the slow, patient shaving of acrylic until the air stopped leaking.\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] [STILLNESS] Three Fridays later, the rain had cleared, and the air along Frenchman Street smelled of wet cedar and fried catfish. Inside The Spotted Cat, the crowd stood shoulder to shoulder near the bar. Marcus Vance stood on the low wooden riser beside Ray Baptiste, the brass horn warm in his hands. [BEAT] He did not have his full range back yet\u2014the high D would wait another month\u2014but when Ray counted off a slow ballad, Marcus lifted the bell, set his lip against the silver rim, and drew a quiet breath. [PAUSE 2s] The first note\u2014a single, round, unadorned middle G\u2014rose cleanly into the rafters, steady as a candle flame. No hiss. No pinch. Just warm brass filling the room. [WARM SMILE] Across town, in a filing cabinet on Canal Street, Marcus Vance's chart still read ninety-four percent. [LOWER VOICE] But as the second chorus opened up and the crowd leaned toward the stage, everyone in the room knew the truth: [EMPHASIS: a metric measures what is easy to count, never what makes the music hold.]",
