      "annotatedScript": "At four-fifteen in the morning, Eighteenth Street in Pilsen was still black with rain. Inside Morales Hearth Bakery, the only light came from the amber glow of the deck ovens and the single fluorescent tube buzzing above the stainless-steel prep table. [PAUSE 1.5s] Elena Morales stood at the scale, her forearms dusted white to the elbows, weighing out eighty-pound batches of sourdough starter. She was fifty-four years old, had started the bakery with two used mixers and a second mortgage, and knew the exact sound each of her four ovens made when the steam valves were about to stick. [BEAT]\n\nEvery Tuesday and Friday, the rhythm was identical. By five-thirty, the delivery vans backed up to the loading bay. By six, fourteen hundred loaves of seeded rye, country boule, and rosemary focaccia were stacked in ventilated plastic crates, bound for forty-two FreshMart Midwest grocery stores across Chicagoland. [EMPHASIS: Sixty-two percent of her ovens] existed solely to feed that contract. It was the anchor that paid the lease, covered health insurance for eleven full-time bakers, and kept the lights on through January. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nAt six-forty, while Elena was wiping down the proofing racks, her phone vibrated against the steel counter. It was an email from FreshMart's regional procurement office. No phone call. No warning. Just a three-paragraph PDF attached to a subject line that read: Vendor Consolidation Notice \u2014 Effective November 1. [LOWER VOICE] FreshMart had signed an exclusive regional agreement with an automated industrial bakery out of Indiana. Morales Hearth had twenty-one days to wind down deliveries. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] By nine o'clock, Elena was sitting in her cramped glass-walled office above the mixing floor, staring at the Q3 profit-and-loss spreadsheet. Outside the window, Mateo, her head baker of nine years, was shaping batards with effortless, rhythmic turns of his wrists. Inside, Elena's cursor blinked on cell G14. Without FreshMart, her quarterly revenue dropped by three hundred and forty thousand dollars. Her fixed overhead\u2014rent, commercial gas, loan payments on the third deck oven\u2014did not drop by a single cent. [BEAT]\n\n[SPEED UP] She called Marcus Vance, the FreshMart category buyer who had toured her bakery two summers earlier and praised her crust on LinkedIn. It took four voicemails before he picked up. [LEAN IN] \"Marcus,\" she said, keeping her voice steady, \"if this is about unit price, give me until Friday. I can sharpen my pencil. I can trim four percent off the wholesale loaf.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nThere was a faint click of a keyboard on the other end of the line. \"Elena, it's not personal,\" Marcus said, his voice flat and rehearsed. \"The new vendor is coming in twelve percent below your base rate, and they're guaranteeing ninety-nine-point-four percent fill-rate compliance across all forty-two doors. My dashboard is red on margin. I don't have room for artisanal variance.\" [BEAT]\n\n\"Twelve percent?\" Elena repeated, her fingers tightening around the receiver. \"Marcus, I pay my bakers twenty-four dollars an hour. I buy stone-ground flour from a mill in Wisconsin. Nobody can bake real bread for twelve percent less unless they're pumping it full of conditioners and freezing it for three weeks.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n\"Our shoppers don't read the ingredient deck at six p.m. on a Wednesday,\" Marcus replied. \"They look at the three-ninety-nine tag. Send me your final October invoice by the twenty-eighth.\" The line went dead. [STILLNESS] To Marcus, eleven livelihoods and nine years of craft were [EMPHASIS: a rounding error on someone else's Tuesday]. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] That night, Elena stayed after the evening crew went home. The bakery was quiet except for the cooling tick of the cast-iron hearths. She printed out twelve months of FreshMart invoices, twelve months of neighborhood cafe orders, and her full general ledger. She spread them across the long maple shaping table, pinning the corners down with brass bench scrapers. [LOWER VOICE] By midnight, her eyes burned. She had tried every combination on her calculator. Cutting three shifts. Switching to cheaper butter. Dropping the seeded rye. None of it closed the gap. [BEAT]\n\nAround one in the morning, she walked out to the loading bay and sat on an overturned milk crate, listening to the rain drum on the awning. For the first time in fourteen years, she wondered if she had built something too stubborn to survive. Maybe the market didn't want a real crust anymore. Maybe she was just a sentimentalist clinging to a dying math problem. [PAUSE 2s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] At four-thirty, Mateo arrived early to fire the ovens. He found Elena asleep with her cheek resting on her forearm at the maple table, surrounded by spreadsheets. He didn't wake her. Instead, he brewed a pot of dark roast, set a chipped mug beside her elbow, and started pulling the morning's batch sheets. When Elena finally blinked awake at five, Mateo was standing across the table, holding a yellow highlighter over one of the printed pages. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n\"You're looking at the wrong column, Jefe,\" Mateo said quietly. [LEAN IN] Elena rubbed her eyes. \"Mateo, I've looked at every column. We lose sixty-two percent of volume on November first.\" [BEAT]\n\nMateo tapped the paper. \"You're looking at gross revenue. Look at [EMPHASIS: the column nobody had highlighted].\" He slid the sheet toward her. It was the credit-memo log\u2014the stale-return deductions FreshMart charged back every month for unsold loaves. \"Every Tuesday and Friday, we send them fourteen hundred loaves,\" Mateo said. \"Every Monday, they deduct eighteen percent for stales. Plus slotting fees. Plus promotional markdowns we had to co-fund. When you subtract what they threw in the dumpster, we weren't making twenty-two percent on FreshMart. We were making three percent.\" [PAUSE 2s]\n\nElena stared at the numbers. Three percent. She had been running her ovens at seventy percent capacity, burning out her mixers, and paying overtime\u2014all to clear [EMPHASIS: a three-percent crust] on a contract that treated her bread like disposable filler. [SLOW DOWN] Then Mateo slid a second sheet beside it. It was the list of twenty-eight independent coffee shops, neighborhood bistros, and corner markets within five miles of Pilsen. \"Zero stale returns,\" Mateo said. \"Zero slotting fees. And last month, six of them asked if we could deliver on Saturdays too, and we told them no because the FreshMart vans were full.\" [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\n[SCENE SHIFT] Elena didn't call Marcus Vance back. Instead, at eight o'clock that morning, she put on a clean apron, walked out to her delivery van, and drove to the first caf\u00e9 on Mateo's list. She didn't bring a spreadsheet. She brought two warm loaves of seeded rye wrapped in butcher paper and a simple one-page wholesale sheet. [WARM SMILE] By Friday, fourteen neighborhood accounts had doubled their standing orders. Three boutique hotels in the West Loop signed on for weekend pastry and bread service. [BEAT]\n\nIt took four months to rebuild the volume, and the bakery was leaner\u2014nine bakers instead of eleven for a brief stretch, until two apprentices came back in the spring. But something fundamental had shifted in the ledger. [EMPHASIS: She stopped pricing for strangers] who measured value in pennies, and started baking for neighbors who measured it in empty baskets. [PAUSE 1.5s]\n\nBy the end of Q2 the following year, Morales Hearth's top-line revenue was still eighteen percent smaller than it had been during the FreshMart peak. Yet net profit was up forty-one percent. [LOWER VOICE] Because [EMPHASIS: the margin was never in the discount]. It was hiding in plain sight, waiting for someone tired enough to stop staring at the biggest number on the page\u2014and finally read the rest of the row. [PAUSE 2s]",
