      "annotatedScript": "[SCENE SHIFT] [WARM SMILE] Every afternoon at two-fifteen, Mateo Delgado parked his white-and-navy ice-cream cart under the shade of the sycamore tree at the corner of Broad Street and Elmwood, three blocks from Rhode Island Hospital. [PAUSE 1.5s] He was fifty-eight years old, had a silver-streaked mustache, and wore a starched linen apron with two deep pockets\u2014one for quarters, one for folded singles. His cart had no digital screen, no QR code, and no loyalty app. It had a brass bell bolted to the handle, a chalkboard with six flavors written in thick yellow chalk, and a heavy insulated lid that smelled faintly of cedar and cold cream. [BEAT] Mateo did not need a dashboard to tell him when the afternoon rush began. He knew it by the sound of the 2:20 bus hissing at the curb, by the way the brick sidewalk held the July heat, and by the exact minute the first wave of nurses in teal scrubs crossed the avenue. [EMPHASIS: Memory was his ledger.] He knew that Clara, the pediatric nurse with the red lanyard, took one scoop of pistachio and one scoop of lemon, never in a sugar cone because she walked while she ate. He knew that Mr. Morales, the retired letter carrier, had a bad left knee on humid Thursdays and always waited by the bench for a small cup of butter pecan. [SLOW DOWN] For nineteen summers, that corner belonged to Mateo. Not because he owned the concrete, but because he remembered the people who stood on it. [PAUSE 1.5s] Then, on the first Monday of June, a sleek matte-black kiosk rolled into the plaza across the street, trailed by two branded electric carts. [LOWER VOICE] Frostline Creamery had arrived. [SCENE SHIFT] Frostline was backed by regional venture capital and ran on [EMPHASIS: forty-two tabs of predictive demand]. Their regional operations lead, a thirty-one-year-old woman named Claire Vance, walked across Broad Street on Tuesday morning with a tablet tucked under her arm. She ordered a single scoop of mint-chip, watched Mateo scoop it, and smiled politely. \"You have a great corner here, Mateo,\" Claire said. \"Our foot-traffic heatmap shows a thirty-eight percent surge between three and four-fifteen. That is why we placed two mobile units on the plaza.\" [BEAT] Mateo wiped his scoop on a clean towel. \"The heatmap is a little fast,\" he said. \"The surge is at three-forty, when the hospital shift changes.\" Claire tapped her screen, amused. \"Our model aggregates cell-phone pings across six blocks. It is pretty precise.\" [SPEED UP] By Friday, Mateo's daily take had dropped by forty percent. Frostline's carts offered twelve flavors, contactless tap-to-pay, and a two-dollar-off flash promotion triggered whenever their algorithm detected a dip in queue length. [LEAN IN] Mateo tried to fight them on their own terms. On Saturday, he stayed open an extra two hours, until nine-thirty at night, hoping to catch the dinner crowd. His feet swelled in his orthopedic shoes, and he sold four cones after eight. On Monday, he cut his prices by fifty cents a scoop to match Frostline's promo. By Wednesday, he was losing money on every waffle cone he handed over. [PAUSE 1.5s] \"You cannot out-discount a venture-backed balance sheet,\" his nephew Tomas told him that evening while helping him hose down the cart's wheels. \"Get a Square reader, Mateo. Put up a sign. Do something.\" Mateo shook his head. \"A sign does not make people cross the street.\" [SCENE SHIFT] [LOWER VOICE] [SLOW DOWN] At eleven-thirty that night, Mateo sat at his small oak kitchen table under a single forty-watt bulb. A half-empty mug of chamomile tea sat beside a stack of damp one-dollar bills. His ledger\u2014a battered composition notebook\u2014lay open to June. The numbers were simple and brutal. Three weeks of this, and he would burn through the emergency fund he kept for his wife's physical therapy. [PAUSE 2s] [STILLNESS] He picked up his pen, stared at the column of figures, and felt a quiet, unfamiliar ache in his chest. For the first time in nineteen years, he wondered if Claire Vance was right. Maybe a man with a brass bell and a chalkboard was just a rounding error waiting to be cleared off the sidewalk. He closed the notebook, turned off the light, and went to bed without setting his alarm. [SCENE SHIFT] [BEAT] The next afternoon, Thursday, started hot and still. At three-oh-five, the sky over Providence bruised dark purple, and a sudden fifteen-minute downpour emptied Broad Street in ninety seconds. Mateo pulled his canvas awning tight and waited under the sycamore. Across the plaza, Frostline's two electric carts hummed, then rolled away toward their depot. Their algorithm had flagged a [EMPHASIS: low-traffic weather event] and pulled the units off the street to save twenty-two dollars in hourly labor. [PAUSE 1.5s] At three-twenty, the rain stopped as abruptly as it had begun. Steam rose off the asphalt. Claire Vance, caught without an umbrella on her way back from a nearby coffee shop, stepped under Mateo's sycamore tree to shake out her jacket. She looked across at the empty plaza, annoyed. \"The system auto-recalled them,\" she muttered, checking her phone. \"Nobody comes out after a three-o'clock storm.\" [LEAN IN] Mateo did not look up from his chalkboard. \"Wait four minutes,\" he said. [BEAT] At three-twenty-five, a side gate at the adjacent park swung open. Twenty-five kids in muddy baseball uniforms, followed by thirty tired parents, spilled onto Broad Street. Mateo did not ask a single one of them what they wanted. [SPEED UP] \"Leo, no sprinkles today, right?\" he called out, already scooping strawberry. \"Mrs. Chen, two cups of vanilla for the twins, and a black cherry for you?\" \"Coach, the usual\u2014double chocolate, extra napkin?\" [WARM SMILE] He served [EMPHASIS: twelve customers in six minutes]. No tablet. No tap-to-pay. Just a brass bell, a steel scoop, and nineteen years of names. [SCENE SHIFT] [SLOW DOWN] Claire Vance stood under the sycamore, her phone still in her hand, watching the line clear. When the last parent walked away, she stepped up to the cart. \"How did you know the Little League game was rained out?\" she asked. Mateo rinsed his scoop in the cold well. \"It was not rained out,\" he said. \"It was a makeup game from Tuesday. They always walk past here at three-twenty-five when the field drains.\" [PAUSE 1.5s] Claire looked at her tablet, then back at Mateo's chalkboard. [EMPHASIS: \"The spreadsheet only knew when people walked past,\" she said quietly. \"It did not know why.\"] Mateo smiled, reached into his apron pocket, and handed her a small napkin. \"Memory is not nostalgia, Claire. It is just data that lives in the person instead of the cloud.\" [BEAT] [STILLNESS] Frostline's carts came back the following week, but Claire never parked them under the sycamore again. Instead, she asked Mateo to consult on their neighborhood training\u2014not to teach them how to scoop, but to teach them how to look up from the screen. And in Frostline's Q3 board deck, right next to the retention curve, there was no heatmap. Just a single photograph of a brass bell.",
